<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=outrigger-canoe">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Transport and navigation</p><h1 class="enc-title">Outrigger canoe</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 3000 BCE</span> · Also known as Outrigger boat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">An outrigger canoe is a boat steadied by one or more side floats, or outriggers, fastened to its hull; such boats were developed by the Austronesian-speaking peoples of the islands of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>, and they enabled the Austronesian expansion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taiwan">Taiwan</a> from around 3000 BCE. Outrigger and double-hulled canoes carried Austronesians east to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-zealand">New Zealand</a> and west across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> as far as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>. Through early Austronesian contact, estimated at around 1000–600 BCE and later, they spread to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and they also reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-africa">East Africa</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/outrigger-canoe.jpg" alt="Outrigger canoe" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Photo Dharma from Penang, Malaysia · CC BY 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borobudur_-_Divyavadana_-_086_N,_The_Landing_of_Hiru_and_foundation_of_Hiruka_(detail_3)_(11706466016).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The simplest ancestral Austronesian boat had five parts: a hollowed-out log as its bottom, two side planks, and two horseshoe-shaped pieces of wood for prow and stern, sewn together with dowels and lashings. It had no central rudder and was steered with an oar on one side, under a mastless triangular crab claw sail with two booms that could be tilted to the wind. Such boats were built as double canoes or with a single outrigger on the windward side.</p><p>Early researchers such as Heine-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geldern">Geldern</a> (1932) and Hornell (1943) held that catamarans evolved from outrigger boats; specialists such as Doran (1981) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahdi">Mahdi</a> (1988) believe the opposite. On their view two logs tied together became the double canoe, then an asymmetric double canoe whose smaller hull turned into the first outrigger, and in Island Southeast Asia the single outrigger later gave way to the double outrigger, or trimaran.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>An outrigger or double-hull vessel draws its stability from the distance between its hulls rather than from the shape of each hull, so its hulls can be longer, narrower and more efficient in the water. Double outriggers, which keep a boat stable when tacking, prevail in Island Southeast Asia, from the small jukung and vinta to the karakoa and kora kora warships. They never reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/micronesia">Micronesia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polynesia">Polynesia</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madagascar">Madagascar</a>, where sailors kept double hulls and reversible single outriggers and developed the technique of shunting. In many Polynesian languages the float is called the ama.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>When Magellan's ships met the Chamorros of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mariana-islands">Mariana Islands</a> in 1521, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antonio-pigafetta">Antonio Pigafetta</a> recorded that their sailboats far surpassed the Spanish ones in speed and manoeuvrability. The priest Francisco Combés wrote in 1667 of the Visayan warships that their builders' care made them &quot;sail like birds, while ours are like lead&quot;. The Sinhalese of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sri-lanka">Sri Lanka</a> call their outrigger fishing canoe the oruwa, and the Tamil, Telugu and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kannada">Kannada</a> words for ship derive from Proto-Hesperonesian *padaw, sailboat.</p><p>Outrigger boats remain in use as fishing and transport vessels; in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> the bangka or paraw is often fitted with a petrol engine, and the barangay, the country's smallest political unit, takes its name from the balangay boats of the first migrations. The Polynesian Voyaging Society sails the double-hulled Hokulea and Hawaiiloa between Pacific islands by traditional navigation without instruments. Outrigger canoe racing is the State sport of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hawaii">Hawaii</a>, where the Molokaʻi Hoe crosses the Kaiwi Channel from Molokai to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oahu</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Outrigger boat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The early Chamorros were expert seafarers, and <b>outrigger canoes</b> served in war, fishing and sailing.</blockquote><a href="/culture/chamorro-people">Chamorro people · Society</a></li><li><blockquote>No Lapita canoe survives, but the speed and reach of their voyages, the Proto-Oceanic words that linguists reconstruct for outriggers, sails and paddles, and the later canoes of Oceania show that they built <b>outrigger canoes</b>, and perhaps double-hulled ones, able to cross hundreds of kilometres of open sea.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lapita-culture">Lapita culture · Arts &amp; Knowledge</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>c. 3000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Island Southeast Asia</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>Outriggers may first have been needed after the crab claw sail appeared, around 1500 BCE.</p><div class="inv-entry-rel"><span class="inv-k">Built on</span><a class="inv-chip" href="/encyclopedia/sail">Sail<span>6th millennium BCE</span></a></div><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=outrigger-canoe">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
